{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "171",
    "case_summary": "A 39-year-old man with a history of treated tuberculosis presented with two weeks of fever, generalized weakness, vague abdominal pain, vomiting, headache, body aches, and moderate nasal bleeding. On examination, he had a 2×1 cm submandibular lymph node and hepatomegaly (7 cm below the costal margin), without gastrointestinal symptoms or skin lesions. Laboratory studies showed urea 18.8 mmol/L, creatinine 141 µmol/L, lipase 182 U/L, myoglobin 108 ng/mL, ferritin >40 000 µg/L, vitamin B12 <111 pmol/L, normal fibrinogen, and triglycerides. Viral serologies were negative. Complete blood count revealed pancytopenia: hemoglobin 6.4 g/dL, WBC 1.7×10^3/µL, ANC 0.9×10^3/µL, platelets 2×10^3/µL. Head CT showed bilateral subdural hemorrhages without midline shift. Whole-body CT demonstrated bilateral pleural effusion, extensive pulmonary consolidation, thrombosis of the celiac artery with splenic infarction, mild hepatomegaly, and massive splenomegaly with infarction. \n\nInitial peripheral smear showed very few atypical cells; a repeat smear revealed marked leukopenia and 13% abnormal blastoid cells of medium size with fine nuclear chromatin, unevenly distributed coarse basophilic granules, and occasional bilobed nuclei. Bone marrow aspirate was hypercellular with 26% immature metachromatic blasts—cells with eccentric nuclei, abundant cytoplasm, and coarse giant granules—as well as some agranular high N/C blasts and spindle-shaped cells. There was trilineage dysplasia with megaloblastic changes in granulocytes and erythroid precursors, dysplastic megakaryocytes, and frequent mitoses. Both histiocytes and the atypical cells demonstrated erythrophagocytosis.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. mast cell leukaemia\nB. Acute myeloid leukaemia was excluded because the blasts exhibited features inconsistent with AML\nC. Acute basophilic leukaemia was considered but ruled out on immunophenotyping\nD. Blastic plasmacytoid dendritic cell neoplasm was excluded by the same immunophenotypic criteria\nE. None\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
